lesson k-light-and-shadow v=1 format=teach grade=0 unit=3 subject=physics levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Light and shadow"
  goal "Say that a shadow is where something blocks the light, find which side it falls, and tell day from night."

  look {
    scene 19x14 {
      shadows stick tall=4 shadow=6 unit=cm lamp=true marks=false at=canvas(1, 1)
    }
    say """
      The lamp shines, and the stick is in the way, so behind the stick the light cannot reach. That
      dark place is a shadow. In the day the sun gives us light; at night the sun is gone and we see
      the moon and the stars.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked k.what-makes-shadow k=0 level=medium
    }
    practice k.what-makes-shadow count=3 seed=2 easy-count=2 hard-count=2
    practice k.shadow-side count=1 seed=3
    show k.day-or-night w=0 hard-w=1
    practice k.shadow-length count=2 seed=4 easy-count=1
    show k.day-or-night w=1 hard-w=0
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show k.shadow-in-the-morning
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show k.shadow-puppet
  }
  remember {
    say "A shadow is where something blocks the light. It falls on the side away from the light."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Read each question aloud. Shadows are best made: a torch, a teddy and a dark room teach this in
    minutes, and so does a sunny morning and a sunny midday. The idea to watch is that a shadow is
    a thing in itself; it is only where light is missing. If the child is stuck, put a hand in the
    torch beam and move it. Off the page, draw round each other's shadows in chalk in the morning
    and again at lunchtime. England, Reception, ELG The Natural World: "Understand some important
    processes and changes in the natural world around them, including the seasons and changing
    states of matter" (recalled, not fetched).
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the sun and a body are worked in the teacher's pen first, the sticks are tall, and the shadows to compare differ a lot."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the questions ask about a room with no light and a glass window, the sticks are short, and the shadows to compare are close."
  }
}
